    correctKey: 'B',
    explanation:
      'Following Rogers v Whitaker, Australian law applies a patient-centred test of materiality: a risk is material if a reasonable person in the patient\'s position would be likely to attach significance to it, or if the practitioner is or should reasonably be aware that this particular patient would attach significance to it. Disclosure is therefore not defined by an incidence threshold or by professional custom. Consent is a process of discussion - covering the nature of the procedure, material risks, benefits, alternatives (including no treatment) and costs - and a signed form is evidence of, but not a substitute for, that discussion.',
